conflicting licenses.","text":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","zbmath":{"author_codes":["frankl.peter"],"document_id":6777683,"msc":["05D05"],"prefix":"Zbl","series_id":7610,"year":"2016"}},{"doi":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","position":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","text":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","zbmath":{"author_codes":["frankl.peter"],"document_id":7063266,"msc":["05D05"],"prefix":"Zbl","series_id":7610,"year":"2019"}},{"doi":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","position":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","text":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","zbmath":{"author_codes":["frankl.peter","furedi.zoltan"],"document_id":3933076,"msc":["05A05","05C35"],"prefix":"Zbl","series_id":479,"year":"1986"}},{"doi":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","position":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","text":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","zbmath":{"author_codes":["tokushige.norihide","frankl.peter"],"document_id":68486,"msc":["05D05","05A20"],"prefix":"Zbl","series_id":479,"year":"1992"}},{"doi":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","position":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","text":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","zbmath":{"author_codes":["patkos.balazs","gerbner.daniel"],"document_id":6828440,"msc":["05-02","05D05","05C35","06A07"],"prefix":"Zbl","series_id":9198,"year":"2019"}},{"doi":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","position":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","text":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","zbmath":{"author_codes":["herzog.jurgen","hibi.takayuki"],"document_id":5790832,"msc":["13-02"],"prefix":"Zbl","series_id":3917,"year":"2011"}},{"doi":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","position":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","text":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","zbmath":{"author_codes":["hilton.anthony-j-w","milner.eric-c"],"document_id":3269902,"msc":["05D05"],"prefix":"Zbl","series_id":649,"year":"1967"}},{"doi":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","position":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","text":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","zbmath":{"author_codes":["hurlbert.glenn-h","kamat.vikram"],"document_id":6861867,"msc":["05D05"],"prefix":"Zbl","series_id":69,"year":"2018"}},{"doi":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","position":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","text":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","zbmath":{"author_codes":["kalai.gil"],"document_id":1795862,"msc":["57Q05","55N33","05E99","52B05","13D03","05D05","55U10","57Q35","57Q15"],"prefix":"Zbl","series_id":0,"year":"2001"}},{"doi":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","position":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","text":"zbMATH Open Web Interface contents unavailable due to conflicting licenses.","zbmath":{"author_codes":["wu.yongjiang","feng.lihua","yu.guihai","liu.jiuqiang","li.yongtao"],"document_id":8093273,"msc":["05D05"],"prefix":"Zbl","series_id":69,"year":"2026"}}],"source":{"book":[],"pages":"603-608","series":[{"acronym":"CMB","issn":[{"number":"0008-4395","type":"print"},{"number":"1496-4287","type":"electronic"}],"issue":"2","issue_id":543382,"parallel_title":"Bulletin Canadien de Mathématiques","part":null,"publisher":"Cambridge University Press, Cambridge; Canadian Mathematical Society, Ottawa, ON","series_id":384,"short_title":"Can. Math. Bull.","title":"Canadian Mathematical Bulletin","volume":"69","year":"2026"}],"source":"Can. Math. Bull. 69, No. 2, 603-608 (2026)"},"states":[["o","has open version"],["o","has open version"],["r","item has references"]],"title":{"addition":null,"original":null,"subtitle":null,"title":"A short proof of the Hilton-Milner theorem"},"year":"2026","zbmath_url":"https://zbmath.org/8209050"},{"biographic_references":[],"contributors":{"authors":[{"aliases":[],"checked":"1","codes":["frankl.peter"],"name":"Frankl, Peter"},{"aliases":[],"checked":"0","codes":["tokushige.norihide"],"name":"Tokushige, Norihide"}],"author_references":[],"editors":[]},"database":"Zbl","datestamp":"2018-09-25T08:07:19Z","document_type":{"code":"b","description":"book / book article"},"editorial_contributions":[{"language":"English","reviewer":{"author_code":"chaudhary.mahendra-pal","reviewer_id":"16528","name":"M. P. Chaudhary","sign":"M. P. Chaudhary (New Delhi)"},"text":"This book is authored by two of the leading researchers in the subject. It presents one of the great appeals of extremal set theory as a subject in which the statements are easily accessible without a lot of mathematical background, yet the ideas and proofs have applications in a wide range of fields including combinatorics, number theory, and probability theory. This book is aimed at mathematically mature undergraduates and highlights the elegance and power of the field of study. The first half of the book provides classic results with some new proofs including a complete proof of the Ahlswede-Khachatrian theorem as well as some recent progress on the Erdős matching conjecture. The second half of the book presents some combinatorial structural results and linear algebra methods including the Deza-Erdős-Frankl theorem, an application of Rödl's packing theorem, an application of semidefinite programming, and very recent progress (obtained in 2016) on the Erdős-Szemeredi sunflower conjecture and capset problem. This book is divided into 32 chapters, each chapter is presented in systematic and understandable ways. The book concludes with a collection of challenging open problems.\n\\par Chapter 1 is devoted to the introduction of combinatorial, mostly extremal problems concerning systems of subsets of a given finite set. In Chapter 2, the authors discuss natural and useful operations on sets and families of subsets, which are also known as sets systems. Chapter 3 is devoted to some almost trivial proofs of classical results. One important result, which was proved independently by three sets of authors namely Perles and Shelah, Sauer, and Vapnik and Chervonensky is also described. In Chapter 4, the Erdős-Ko-Rado theorem via shifiting is presented. Kindly note that this is one of the oldest results in extremal set theory, a theorem that still influences a good part of the ongoing research in this field. In Chapter 5, the basic ideas and properties of Katona's circle are explained. An alternative proof of the Erdős-Ko-Rado theorem using Katona's circle is presented. The Lubell-Yamamoto-Meshalkin (LYM) inequality [\\textit{D. Lubell}, J. Comb. Theory 1, 299 (1966; Zbl 0151.01503); \\textit{K. Yamamoto}, J. Math. Soc. Japan 6, 343--353 (1954; Zbl 0056.26301); \\textit{L. D. Meshalkin}, Theory Probab. Appl. 8, 203--204 (1963; Zbl 0123.36303); translation from Teor. Veroyatn. Primen. 8, 219--220 (1963)] is presented.\n\\par Chapter 6 is devoted to the Kruskal-Katona theorem which has many applications outside extremal set theory. In Chapter 7, a short proof of an important classic result of \\textit{D. J. Kleitman} [J. Comb. Theory 5, 157--163 (1968; Zbl 0245.05003)] is presented. Chapter 8 is devoted to the Hilton-Milner theorem [\\textit{A. J. W. Hilton} and \\textit{E. C. Milner}, Q. J. Math., Oxf. II. Ser. 18, 369--384 (1967; Zbl 0168.26205)], and also discusses its latest proof given by Frankl. Chapter 9 is devoted to the Erdős matching conjecture [\\textit{P. Erdős}, Ann. Univ. Sci. Budap. Rolando Eötvös, Sect. Math. 8, 93--96 (1965; Zbl 0136.21302)], its proof is discussed in brief. Chapter 10--11 are devoted to the Ahlswede-Khachatrian theorem and its two proofs, one based on the method of generating sets and the other using the pushing-pulling method [\\textit{R. Ahlswede} and \\textit{L. H. Khachatrian}, Eur. J. Comb. 18, No. 2, 125--136 (1997; Zbl 0869.05066); Combinatorica 19, No. 1, 1--15 (1999; Zbl 0980.05049)], both approaches are purely combinatorial and may be considered ``dual'' to each other in some sense. Those readers who are interested to know more about the Ahlswede and Khachatrian theorem may consult the survey written by \\textit{C. Bey} and \\textit{K. Engel} [in: Numbers, information and complexity. Dedicated to Rudolf Ahlswede on the occasion of his 60th birthday. Dordrecht: Kluwer Academic Publishers. 45--74 (2000; Zbl 1122.05316)].\n\\par Chapter 12 is devoted to uniform measure versus product measure, which are closely related to each other. The Erdős-Ko-Rado theorem (EKR) is described and proved on the lines of uniform measure and product measure by \\textit{R. Ahlswede} and \\textit{G. O. H. Katona} [Discrete Math. 17, 1--22 (1977; Zbl 0368.05001)] and \\textit{Y. Filmus} [``Erdős-Ko-Rado for \\(\\mu_p\\) using Katona's circle method'', Preprint, \\url{http://www.cs.toronto.edu/~yuvalf/pEKR.pdf}]. Several related results are also discussed. Chapter 13 is devoted to \\textit{D. J. Kleitman}'s correlation inequality [J. Comb. Theory 1, 209--214 (1966; Zbl 0148.01105)], and recent results are presented. In Chapter 14, the authors discuss about $r$-cross union families and the extension of the EKR theorem given by \\textit{P. Frankl} and \\textit{N. Tokushige} [Comb. Probab. Comput. 20, No. 5, 749--752 (2011; Zbl 1283.05259)]. In Chapter 15, some simple applications of the random walk method in extremal problems for finite sets are described. The first serious application of the random walk method is due to \\textit{P. Frankl} [in: Combinatorics, Keszthely 1976, Colloq. Math. Soc. Janos Bolyai 18, 365--375 (1978; Zbl 0401.05001)], and it is applicable to cross $t$-interesting families, $r$-wise $t$-interesting families etc. [\\textit{P. Frankl} et al., J. Comb. Theory, Ser. A 128, 207--249 (2014; Zbl 1301.05316), \\textit{S. J. Lee} et al., Discrete Appl. Math. 216, Part 3, 627--645 (2017; Zbl 1358.05298)].\n\\par Chapter 16--17 are devoted to $L$-systems or $(K,L)$ systems, the authors introduce their concept and rank, and present fundamental results. P. Frankl showed that, for every rational number $q\\ge 1$ there are infinitely many choices of $K$ and $L$ such that $\\alpha(K, L)= q$ [J. Comb. Theory, Ser. A 42, 200--206 (1986; Zbl 0603.05001)]. The main idea of this theorem is described in Chapter 17. The general upper bound for $m(n,k,L)$ due to \\textit{M. Deza} et al. [Proc. Lond. Math. Soc. (3) 36, 369--384 (1978; Zbl 0407.05006)] is also discussed and the Füredi structure theorem [\\textit{Z. Füredi}, Discrete Math. 47, 129--132 (1983; Zbl 0531.05002)] is shown. Lastly, the authors deal with the exponent of a $(10,\\{0,1,3,6\\})$-system, its properties and related proofs. Chapter 18 is devoted to the DEF theorem. The analogue results to DEF and its related properties and subsequent proofs are presented. Chapter 19 describes the proof of the Füredi structure theorem using properties of hypergraphs, and also related results given by \\textit{P. Erdős} and \\textit{D. J. Kleitman} [J. Comb. Theory 5, 164--169 (1968; Zbl 0167.22302)]. In Chapter 20, the authors consider sufficient conditions for a hypergraph to have an almost perfect matching and suggest two applications such as Rödl's packing theorem and the construction of a larger $L$-system whose intersection structure is a Steiner system. The detailed description of \\textit{V. Rödl}'s packing theorem [Eur. J. Comb. 6, 69--78 (1985; Zbl 0565.05016)] and the extended technique to prove this theorem given by \\textit{P. Frankl} and \\textit{V. Rödl} [Eur. J. Comb. 6, 317--326 (1985; Zbl 0624.05055)] are shown. The Rödl-Tengan construction of an $L$-system on a Steiner system [\\textit{V. Rödl} and \\textit{E. Tengan},J. Comb. Theory, Ser. A 113, No. 6, 1214--1218 (2006; Zbl 1098.05078)] and related properties are described in detail.\n\\par Chapter 21 is devoted to upper bounds using multilinear polynomials. The authors describe \\textit{D. K. Ray-Chaudhuri} and \\textit{R. M. Wilson}'s theorem [Osaka J. Math. 12, 737--744 (1975; Zbl 0342.05018)], \\textit{P. Frankl} and \\textit{R. M. Wilson}'s theorem [Combinatorica 1, 357--368 (1981; Zbl 0498.05048)], \\textit{M. Deza} et al.'s theorem [Combinatorica 3, 331--339 (1983; Zbl 0528.05012)], and \\textit{H. S. Snevily}'s theorem [Combinatorica 23, No. 3, 527--533 (2003; Zbl 1045.05087)]. Chapter 22 is devoted to applications to discrete geometry. In some problems of discrete geometry, the geometric constraints can be expressed in terms of intersections in hypergraphs, then such problems may be solved by applying the corresponding results on $L$-system. The findings by \\textit{R. A. Fisher} [Ann. Eugenics 10, 52--75 (1940; Zbl 0063.01383)], \\textit{X. Chen} and \\textit{V. Chvátal} [Discrete Appl. Math. 156, No. 11, 2101--2108 (2008; Zbl 1157.05019)], and \\textit{P. Frankl} and \\textit{R. M. Wilson} [Combinatorica 1, 357--368 (1981; Zbl 0498.05048)] are also discussed. Chapter 23 is devoted to upper bounds using inclusion matrics. The authors present recent findings due to Frankl and Wilson [loc. cit.], and an extension of these results to non-uniform families by \\textit{L. Babai} and \\textit{P. Frankl} [Linear algebra methods in combinatorics. II. University of Chicago, Department of Computer Science (1992)]. Further, \\textit{G. Katona}'s intersecting shadow theorem [Acta Math. Acad. Sci. Hung. 15, 329--337 (1964; Zbl 0134.25101)] and its related properties are discussed. Chapter 24 is devoted to some algebraic constructions for $L$-systems. Please note that designs, codes, and finite geometrics are useful sources for the construction for large $L$-systems. The basic definitions, examples and properties of algebraic constructions are explained. The valuable findings by \\textit{M. Deza} et al. [Proc. Lond. Math. Soc. (3) 50, 405--425 (1985; Zbl 0549.05006)] and \\textit{N. Tokushige} [J. Comb. Theory, Ser. A 113, No. 3, 420--434 (2006; Zbl 1088.05073)] are presented. Chapter 25 is devoted to oddtown and eventown problems. The eventown/oddtown theorems were proved independently by \\textit{E. R. Berlekamp} [Can. Math. Bull. 12, 471--474 (1969; Zbl 0272.05004)] and \\textit{J. E. Graver} [Linear Algebra Appl. 10, 111--128 (1975; Zbl 0301.05012)]. Also, \\textit{P. Frankl} and \\textit{N. Tokushige} [Eur. J. Comb. 51, 280--286 (2016; Zbl 1321.05015)] have discussed uniform eventown problems.\n\\par Chapter 26 is devoted to the tensor product method. The authors present a literature review based on the works of prior researchers and present basic terminology such as multilinear map, tensor product, symmetric tensors, alternating tensors, symmetric algebra, exterior algebra etc. The applications of symmetric products, alternating products are due to \\textit{B. Bollobás} [Acta Math. Acad. Sci. Hung. 16, 447--452 (1965; Zbl 0138.19404)], \\textit{L. Lovácz} [Graph theory and related topics, Proc. Conf. Honour W. T. Tutte, Waterloo/Ont. 1977, 1--14 (1979; Zbl 0462.05037)], \\textit{P. Frankl} [Eur. J. Comb. 3, 125--127 (1982; Zbl 0488.05004)], and \\textit{Z. Füredi} [Eur. J. Comb. 5, 133--136 (1984; Zbl 0546.05048)]. Chapter 27 is devoted to the ratio bound. The Erdős-Ko-Rado theorem is proved using this concept. The ratio bound gives an upper bound for graph invariants using eigenvalues of an adjacency matrix. The independence number is one of the most important graph invariants. The bounding of the independence number by a positive semidefinite matrix is presented. The ratio bound theorem was proved independently by \\textit{A. J. Hoffman} [Graph Theory Appl., Proc. advanced Sem. Wisconsin, Madison 1969, 79--91 (1970; Zbl 0221.05061)] and \\textit{P. Delsarte} [An algebraic approach to the association schemes of coding theory. Ann Arbor, MI: Historical Jrl. (1973; Zbl 1075.05606)]. The applications of the ratio bound are describe based on the findings of \\textit{R. M. Wilson} [Combinatorica 4, 247--257 (1984; Zbl 0556.05039)]. Chapter 28 is devoted to measures of cross independent sets, where the ratio bound (as given in Chapter 27) is extended into two ways by considering, (i) the measure of an independent set instead of an independence number, and (ii) cross independent sets in a bipartite graph, which are a natural expansion of independent sets in a graph. The idea of bounding measures using singular values and singular decomposition, the extension of the ratio bound and its applications are presented. Chapter 29 is devoted to the application of semidefinite programming (an extension of linear programming), which is a strong tool for some combinatorial problems. The semidefinite programming is useful for measure of cross independent sets. The findings by \\textit{S. Suda} and \\textit{H. Tanaka} [Bull. Lond. Math. Soc. 46, No. 2, 342--348 (2014; Zbl 1285.05181)] and \\textit{S. Suda} et al. [Math. Program. 166, No. 1--2 (A), 113--130 (2017; Zbl 1375.05261)] are presented. Chapter 30 is devoted to the cross intersection problem with measures, which is actually a continuation of Chapter 29. The findings by \\textit{P. C. Fishburn} et al. [SIAM J. Algebraic Discrete Methods 7, 73--79 (1986; Zbl 0582.60014)], and its extension to two families by Suda, Tanaka and Tokushige [loc. cit.] are presented.\n\\par Chapter 31 is devoted to capsets and sunflowers. The recent developments of a polynomial method (which is also known as slice rank method), and its two applications are discussed. A result on slice rank given by \\textit{T. Tao} [``A symmetric formulation of the Croot-Lev-Pach-Ellenberg-Gijswijt capset bound'', \\url{http://terrytao.wordpress.com/2016/05/18/a}] is presented. The authors also describe the proofs for results obtained by \\textit{J. S. Ellenberg} and \\textit{D. Gijswijt} [Ann. Math. (2) 185, No. 1, 339--343 (2017; Zbl 1425.11020)] and the conjecture introduced by \\textit{P. Erdős} and \\textit{E. Szemerédi} [J. Comb. Theory, Ser. A 24, 308--313 (1978; Zbl 0383.05002)]. Chapter 32 includes several challenging open problems such as the Chvátal conjecture, Frankl's union-closed conjecture, maximal families without $s+1$ pairwise disjoint sets, the Erdős matching conjecture, the Kleitman matching problem, and a diversity of interesting hypergraphs and missing intersections.","contribution_type":"review"}],"id":6943280,"identifier":"1416.05001","keywords":["vertex degrees","extremal set theory","finite sets","upper bound","ratio bound","cross independent sets","tensor product","multilinear polynomials","packing and covering","combinatorics","number theory","probability theory","Katona's circle","$L$-system","Steiner system","semidefinite programming","pairwise disjoint sets","Kleitman matching problem"],"language":{"languages":["English"],"addition":[null]},"license":[],"links":[{"identifier":"10.1090/stml/086","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1090/stml/086"}],"msc":[{"code":"05-01","scheme":"msc2020","text":"Introductory exposition (textbooks, tutorial papers, etc.) pertaining to combinatorics"},{"code":"05D05","scheme":"msc2020","text":"Extremal set theory"},{"code":"05B40","scheme":"msc2020","text":"Combinatorial aspects of packing and covering"},{"code":"05B07","scheme":"msc2020","text":"Triple systems"},{"code":"90C22","scheme":"msc2020","text":"Semidefinite programming"}],"references":[],"source":{"book":[{"book_id":6943280,"isbn":[{"number":"978-1-4704-4039-8","type":"pbk"},{"number":"978-1-4704-4847-9","type":"ebook"}],"publisher":"Providence, RI: American Mathematical Society (AMS)","year":"2018"}],"pages":"viii, 224~p.","series":[{"acronym":null,"issn":[{"number":"1520-9121","type":"print"},{"number":"2376-9173","type":"electronic"}],"issue":null,"issue_id":414671,"parallel_title":null,"part":null,"publisher":"American Mathematical Society (AMS), Providence, RI","series_id":4620,"short_title":"Stud. Math. Libr.","title":"Student Mathematical Library","volume":"86","year":"2018"}],"source":"Student Mathematical Library 86. Providence, RI: American Mathematical Society (AMS) (ISBN 978-1-4704-4039-8/pbk; 978-1-4704-4847-9/ebook). viii, 224~p. (2018)."},"states":[["c","is cited"]],"title":{"addition":null,"original":null,"subtitle":null,"title":"Extremal problems for finite sets"},"year":"2018","zbmath_url":"https://zbmath.org/6943280"},{"biographic_references":[],"contributors":{"authors":[{"aliases":[],"checked":"0","codes":["kalimulina.elmira-yu"],"name":"El'mira Yu. Kalimulina"}],"author_references":[],"editors":[]},"database":"arXiv","datestamp":"0001-01-01T00:00:00Z","document_type":{"code":null,"description":null},"editorial_contributions":[{"language":null,"reviewer":{"author_code":null,"reviewer_id":null,"name":null,"sign":null},"text":"Summary: Let $D_n$ be the disjointness graph on the nonempty subsets of $[n]$, whose independent sets are exactly the intersecting families on $[n]$. We study the weighted independent-set polynomial $W(n)=\\sum_F\\prod_{S\\in F}w(S)$, the sum running over these families, for the doubly exponential weight $w(S)=2^{2^{n-|S|}}-1$. The kernel-bearing (trivial) part $Z_\\cap(n)$ is exact by inclusion-exclusion and satisfies $Z_\\cap(n)\\sim n\\cdot 2^{3^{n-1}}$. For the kernel-free remainder we prove the exact prefactor $R(n)=(3/4+o(1))n\\cdot 2^{3^{n-1}-2^{n-1}+2}$, whence $\\log_2(Z_\\cap(n)/R(n))=2^{n-1}-2+\\log_2(4/3)+o(1)$, an additive $o(1)$, not merely a leading-order one. The engine is a second-level extremal theorem: among kernel-free maximal linked systems other than the $n$ one-flip stars, the largest weight exponent is $3^{n-1}-3\\cdot 2^{n-2}+6$, a fixed gap $2^{n-2}-4$ below the maximum, with the extremisers classified exactly. None of this is special to the weight: for $w_B(S)=B^{B^{n-|S|}}-1$ with integer $B\\ge 2$ the same stars dominate, the near-extremal families sit a gap $B^{n-2}-B^2$ below, and the prefactor is $1-B^{-B}$. The combinatorial input is the $p$-biased extremal problem for non-trivial intersecting families: $M_2(n,p)=p-pq^{n-1}+qp^{n-1}$ for all $n\\ge 3$, $0<p\\le 1/2$, $q=1-p$. This first level is essentially known: the extremal family is the Wheel coterie of Peleg and Wool, and at $p=1/Q$ the statement, with its maximiser classification, is the case $r=n$ of Borg's Hilton-Milner theorem for signed sets (2013). We give a short self-contained Erdős-Ko-Rado proof, uniform in real $p\\in(0,1/2]$, whose layer-two rigidity feeds the second level. The novelty claimed lies at the second level and in the prefactor, where the classification cannot be read off the layer profile alone: at $n=5$ one profile carries two non-isomorphic types of extremisers.","contribution_type":"summary"}],"id":903107361,"identifier":"arXiv:2607.16040","keywords":[],"language":{"languages":[],"addition":[null]},"license":["http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"],"links":[{"identifier":"2607.16040","type":"arxiv","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.16040"}],"msc":[{"code":"05D05","scheme":"msc2020","text":"Extremal set theory"},{"code":"05A16","scheme":"msc2020","text":"Asymptotic enumeration"},{"code":"05C69","scheme":"msc2020","text":"Vertex subsets with special properties (dominating sets, independent sets, cliques, etc.)"},{"code":"82B20","scheme":"msc2020","text":"Lattice systems (Ising, dimer, Potts, etc.) and systems on graphs arising in equilibrium statistical mechanics"}],"references":[],"source":{"book":[],"pages":null,"series":[],"source":"Preprint, arXiv:2607.16040 [math.CO] (2026)"},"states":[["o","has open version"],["s","item with single author"]],"title":{"addition":null,"original":null,"subtitle":null,"title":"A gap theorem for non-trivial maximal intersecting families and an exact weighted asymptotic"},"year":"2026","zbmath_url":"https://zbmath.org/903107361"}],"status":{"execution":"successful request","execution_bool":true,"internal_code":"ok","last_id":null,"nr_total_results":6,"nr_request_results":6,"query_execution_time_in_seconds":0.34765005111694336,"status_code":200,"time_stamp":"2026-11-21T07:58:10Z"}}